\details{
\strong{Variance fraction.} For a continuous outcome, the signal split
corresponds to an R-squared split when the components are orthogonal in the
population. For binary and survival outcomes the metric split is only
approximate because AUC and concordance also depend on their outcome links.
The fixed reference estimates target population orthogonality rather than
enforcing exact orthogonality in each generated dataset.

\strong{Construction.} The linear predictor is
\deqn{lp = \alpha + \beta\,\Bigl(\,\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_j x_j}_{L}
       \; + \; \kappa\,\mathrm{sd}(L)\, \underbrace{\tilde N}_{N\,\mathrm{std}} \Bigr)}
where \eqn{L = \sum_j x_j} is the linear score (identical to complexity 1)
and \eqn{\tilde N} is the nonlinear aggregate (\eqn{\sum_j x_j^2} for C2;
plus \eqn{\sum_{j<k} x_j x_k} for C3) \emph{residualised against the full
design} \eqn{[1, x_1, \ldots, x_S]} and standardised to unit SD. The
projection coefficients and both SDs are fixed estimates, computed
once per predictor configuration from a 500,000-row reference draw (see
\code{nonlinear_reference()}), so every row is generated by the same model
whatever the number of rows drawn. With exact population projection and
scaling constants, the nonlinear variance fraction would be
\deqn{f = \frac{\kappa^2}{1 + \kappa^2},\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad
      \kappa = \sqrt{\frac{f}{1 - f}}.}
The user supplies \code{nonlinear_strength} = \eqn{f} (e.g. 0.2 = 20\% of the
signal variance is nonlinear) and \eqn{\kappa} is computed internally. The
population fraction is approximately \eqn{f}. Finite-reference estimation
error means exact orthogonality and variance shares are not guaranteed.
Approximation error can be larger for heavy-tailed predictors such as t
and lognormal, particularly after squaring.

Because the bracket is fixed given X, \code{lp} is linear in
\code{beta_signal}, so the tuning routines (which scale \code{beta_signal} to
hit the target oracle AUC / C-index) work unchanged.

\strong{Calibrating difficulty.} \eqn{f} sets the difficulty order but not, by
itself, a precise AUC/C-index gap: the metric reduction a given \eqn{f}
